Complete Terraform Command Reference – Beginner to Advanced
This is a Terraform command cheat sheet for your Azure Infrastructure as Code training. It covers basic commands, resource deployment, state file management, variables, modules, workspaces, troubleshooting, and production workflows.
1. Terraform basic commands
Command	Purpose
terraform version	Display Terraform version
terraform -help	Display general help
terraform -help plan	Help for a specific command
terraform init	Initialize Terraform working directory
terraform fmt	Format Terraform configuration files
terraform validate	Validate configuration
terraform console	Open interactive expression console
terraform providers	Display required providers
terraform graph	Generate dependency graph
terraform metadata	Display Terraform metadata subcommands

Practical examples:
terraform version
terraform -help
terraform -help apply
terraform fmt
terraform fmt -recursive
terraform validate
terraform providers


2. Terraform initialization commands
These commands are used before deploying infrastructure.
Command	Purpose
terraform init	Initialize Terraform
terraform init -upgrade	Upgrade provider selections within constraints
terraform init -reconfigure	Reconfigure backend
terraform init -migrate-state	Migrate existing state to a new backend
terraform init -backend=false	Initialize without configuring backend
terraform init -get=false	Skip downloading modules

Examples:
terraform init
terraform init -upgrade
terraform init -reconfigure
terraform init -migrate-state


3. Terraform plan commands
Used to preview changes before creating or modifying infrastructure.
Command	Purpose
terraform plan	Preview infrastructure changes
terraform plan -out=tfplan	Save execution plan
terraform plan -var="environment=dev"	Pass variable through CLI
terraform plan -var-file="dev.tfvars"	Use a specific variable file
terraform plan -refresh-only	Preview state refresh
terraform plan -target=RESOURCE	Target a specific resource
terraform plan -destroy	Preview resource destruction
terraform show tfplan	Inspect saved plan

Examples:
terraform plan

terraform plan -out=gopal.tfplan

terraform plan -var-file="dev.tfvars"

terraform plan -var="environment=prod"

terraform plan -destroy


4. Terraform apply commands
Used to create and update infrastructure.
Command	Purpose
terraform apply	Apply configuration changes
terraform apply -auto-approve	Apply without interactive approval
terraform apply tfplan	Apply a saved plan
terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"	Apply using variable file
terraform apply -replace=RESOURCE	Replace a resource
terraform apply -target=RESOURCE	Apply changes targeting a resource
terraform apply -refresh-only	Update state from actual infrastructure

Examples:
terraform apply

terraform apply -auto-approve

terraform apply gopal.tfplan

terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

terraform apply -replace='azurerm_linux_virtual_machine.web_vm["web1"]'


Production caution
Avoid routinely using -target or -auto-approve in production. Review the complete execution plan before applying changes.
5. Terraform destroy commands
Used to remove infrastructure managed by Terraform.
Command	Purpose
terraform destroy	Destroy managed infrastructure
terraform destroy -auto-approve	Destroy without interactive approval
terraform plan -destroy	Preview destruction
terraform destroy -target=RESOURCE	Destroy a targeted resource
terraform apply -destroy	Alternative destroy workflow

Examples:
terraform destroy

terraform plan -destroy

terraform destroy -auto-approve

terraform destroy -target=azurerm_resource_group.my_rg


Remember: Destroying an Azure Resource Group can also delete its contained resources. Review the plan carefully.

6. Terraform state file commands
Very important for your Azure training
The state file maintains the mapping between Terraform resource addresses and actual Azure resources.
Command	Purpose
terraform state list	List all managed resources
terraform state show RESOURCE	Display resource details
terraform state pull	Download current state
terraform state push FILE	Upload state file
terraform state mv OLD NEW	Move or rename resource address
terraform state rm RESOURCE	Remove resource from state
terraform state replace-provider	Replace provider source in state
terraform show	Display current state or saved plan

Examples using your Azure resources:
# List all resources
terraform state list

# Inspect Resource Group
terraform state show azurerm_resource_group.my_rg

# Inspect Virtual Network
terraform state show azurerm_virtual_network.vnet

# Inspect subnet
terraform state show azurerm_subnet.web_subnet

# Inspect first VM
terraform state show 'azurerm_linux_virtual_machine.web_vm["web1"]'

# Inspect second VM
terraform state show 'azurerm_linux_virtual_machine.web_vm["web2"]'

# Download state
terraform state pull

# Display state
terraform show


Rename a resource reference:
terraform state mv \
azurerm_resource_group.my_rg \
azurerm_resource_group.gopal_rg


Remove a resource from Terraform state without deleting Azure infrastructure:
terraform state rm azurerm_resource_group.my_rg


Be careful: state rm does not destroy the Azure resource. Terraform will no longer track that resource at the removed address.
7. Terraform state refresh and drift commands
Command	Purpose
terraform plan -refresh-only	Detect and preview state differences
terraform apply -refresh-only	Update state from actual infrastructure
terraform refresh	Legacy refresh command; deprecated
terraform plan	Refresh and compare infrastructure
terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID	Release a state lock when safe

Examples:
# Detect infrastructure drift
terraform plan

# Preview state refresh
terraform plan -refresh-only

# Update state
terraform apply -refresh-only

# Unlock state (only after confirming no active operation)
terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID


8. Terraform variable commands
These commands help manage input variables.
Command	Purpose
terraform plan -var	Pass a variable
terraform apply -var	Pass a variable during apply
terraform plan -var-file	Load a variable file
terraform apply -var-file	Apply with variable file
terraform console	Evaluate variable expressions

Examples:
terraform plan -var="environment=dev"

terraform plan -var="location=eastus"

terraform plan -var-file="dev.tfvars"

terraform plan -var-file="prod.tfvars"

terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"


Variable precedence generally follows this order, from lower to higher:
1. Variable defaults.
2. terraform.tfvars and terraform.tfvars.json.
3. *.auto.tfvars files.
4. -var-file and -var command-line arguments, with later CLI arguments taking precedence.
9. Terraform output commands
Used to retrieve information exposed through output blocks.
Command	Purpose
terraform output	Display all outputs
terraform output RESOURCE	Display a specific output
terraform output -json	Display outputs in JSON
terraform output -raw NAME	Display raw output value

Examples:
terraform output

terraform output resource_group_name

terraform output vnet_name

terraform output -json

terraform output -raw resource_group_location


For your two Linux VMs:
terraform output linux_vm_details

terraform output linux_vm_public_ips


10. Terraform workspace commands
Workspaces allow multiple state instances for the same Terraform configuration.
Command	Purpose
terraform workspace list	List workspaces
terraform workspace show	Show current workspace
terraform workspace new dev	Create workspace
terraform workspace select dev	Select workspace
terraform workspace delete dev	Delete workspace
terraform workspace select -or-create dev	Select or create workspace

Examples:
terraform workspace list

terraform workspace show

terraform workspace new dev

terraform workspace new staging

terraform workspace new prod

terraform workspace select dev

terraform workspace select prod


Example workspace architecture
dev

dev state


staging

staging state


prod

prod state



Each workspace maintains a separate state for the same configuration and backend.

11. Terraform resource import commands
Import existing Azure resources into Terraform management.
Command	Purpose
terraform import ADDRESS ID	Import an existing resource
terraform plan	Verify imported resource configuration
terraform state show ADDRESS	Inspect imported resource

Example:
terraform import \
azurerm_resource_group.my_rg \
/subscriptions/SUBSCRIPTION_ID/resourceGroups/gopal-rg


Important: Terraform import updates state, but it does not automatically generate your complete .tf configuration. You must write the corresponding resource configuration yourself.

Terraform also supports configuration-driven import using an import block:
import {
  to = azurerm_resource_group.my_rg
  id = "/subscriptions/SUBSCRIPTION_ID/resourceGroups/gopal-rg"
}


Then:
terraform plan
terraform apply


